{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## /rebar-debug RCA — investigated, AC#1+AC#4 delivered, AC#2 rests on a false premise\n\n**Confirmed mechanism (defect, as the ticket states).** The idempotency oracle froze\n`_now_iso` only on `binding_store`. RP-02 S2 T2 moved the absence/tombstone/comment\ncluster (`clear_absent` + siblings) into `binding_lifecycle`, which resolves its OWN\nmodule-level `_now_iso` (binding_lifecycle.py:45; `clear_absent` at :296 stamps via it).\nSo the freeze no longer reaches those moved ops. **Fix (AC#1):** freeze BOTH module\nclocks. Delivered + verified (idempotency file 3/3 green, no regression).\n\n**AC#4 audit (done):** the only `binding_store_mod`-targeted patch of a moved subject in\n`test_reconcile_idempotency.py` is the `_now_iso` freeze at line 311. No other moved-subject\npatches exist. Clean.\n\n**AC#2 is NOT satisfiable through this integration test — verified at runtime, not assumed.**\nAC#2 asks: with the patch in place, seeding an unconditional `clear_absent` stamp must drive\n`test_reconcile_idempotency.py` RED. It cannot, for two independently sufficient reasons:\n\n1. **`clear_absent` is never reached on a healthy in-window pass.** Its only two call sites —\n   `binding_walk.py:254` (`persist and obs.state is PRESENT and jira_key not in curr_snapshot`)\n   and `outbound_differ.py:675` (bound-but-absent key whose direct GET returns 200) — are\n   BOTH gated on an *out-of-window-but-alive* key. The idempotency fixture seeds all keys\n   in-window, so `clear_absent` does not run. Proof: instrumented entry trace over both\n   passes shows **zero** `clear_absent` calls; a seeded unconditional-stamp mutation left the\n   test GREEN.\n2. **Even forcing a key out-of-window, the differ's direct GET does not hit the in-process\n   fake.** The fetch uses `fetcher._load_acli` (the fake), but the bound-but-absent GET uses\n   `run_differs._load_reconcile_backend()` → `select_backend(compose_config())`, a\n   config-resolved REAL backend whose `.transport` 404s for a synthetic key. So a forced\n   out-of-window `DIG-1` takes the 404 → `note_absent_or_rekey` path, never `clear_absent`.\n   Proof: with an added `out_of_window` fake mechanism, the differ selected `DIG-1` for a GET\n   (`SELECTED={'DIG-1'}`) but `_safe_get_issue` returned `_DELETED` (404) and the fake's\n   `get_issue_by_rest` was never called.\n\nThe `clear_absent` no-stamp contract is already pinned at unit tier:\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_lifecycle.py::test_clear_absent_does_not_dirty_an_entry_with_no_absence`\n(the ticket's own Impact note acknowledges this containment). The AC#2 premise — that a\nhealthy idempotent pass exercises `clear_absent` — is factually wrong for this test.\n\n**Recommendation (for user/reviewer).** Either (a) accept AC#1 hardening + the existing\nunit-tier guard as sufficient and drop/reclassify AC#2; or (b) if an integration-tier\nmutation-RED is still wanted, it requires a harness extension that wires the fake into the\nBackend port (`.transport` + `.outbound`/`.inbound` mappers) AND an out-of-window fixture so\nthe bound-but-absent-alive path executes — that is a test-harness story of its own, larger\nthan this follow-up's framing. Filing as a follow-up is the recommended path if (b) is\ndesired."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787164346002065001,"uuid":"9b9aa5db-8807-4e10-9521-e4fd9df49ae3"}